deep.reconstruction:
Context (pp. 30–32). L is a 'réseau' equipped with cuttings ('découpages'). A tame ('modérée') part X = X_Φ is given by a finite, linearly ordered, mutually separated family of slices (pseudo-tranches) T_1 ≪ T_2 ≪ … ≪ T_n. These are the connected components of X, so Card π0(X) = n. On p.30 Grothendieck defines the tame complement X' = C_mod(X): the set of points of L∖X in tame position relative to X. He identifies it as the largest tame part disjoint from X and in relative tame position to it. X' is described by n−1 interstitial slices (between T_i and T_{i+1}), plus a left interstitial slice before T_1 and a right one after T_n when these exist.

This page finishes that count. Let n' be the number of slices in the cutting Φ' ∈ Déc^α_cc(Fr(L)) defining X'. Then n' = (n−1) + ε_left + ε_right, where:
- ε_left = 1 iff T_1 is bounded below (minoré);
- ε_right = 1 iff T_n is bounded above (majoré).

So n' ∈ {n−1, n, n+1} and |Card π0(X) − Card π0(X')| ≤ 1. Precisely:
- T_1 not bounded below and T_n not bounded above: n' = n−1;
- exactly one end bounded: n' = n;
- both bounded: n' = n+1.

This is the elementary count of complementary intervals of a finite union of disjoint intervals in a line, done in the abstract slice setting.

Second part: a programme. Grothendieck considers M = Mod(L), the set of tame parts of L, with three structures:
- (a) the inclusion order;
- (b) the symmetric relation '{X,Y} in relative tame position', with X ⊂ Y ⇒ {X,Y} tame;
- (c) the subset M_f = Mod_fr(L) of 'franche' (frank) tame parts, i.e. those whose slices are frank pseudo-slices.

A margin note asks whether relation (b) is determined by the order, e.g. whether {X,Y} is tame iff X∧Y exists.

The next page lists the properties:
- bounded poset;
- meets and joins exist for compatible pairs;
- X ↦ X' is an order-reversing involution, characterised as the unique compatible complement;
- De Morgan laws for compatible pairs;
- a closure X ↦ X̄ onto frank parts, which preserves compatible joins.

In modern terms this is a bounded poset with a compatibility relation, an orthocomplementation and a closure operator: an orthoposet / partial Boolean algebra–like structure abstracted from tame subsets of a 1-dimensional ordered space.
